Answer an LIR for an identity the HSS does not have with
DIAMETER_ERROR_USER_UNKNOWN instead of SERVER_NAME_NOT_STORED.

hss_ogs_diam_cx_lir_cb() looks the public identity up with
hss_cx_get_server_name() and, on NULL, answers Experimental-Result-Code 2004
(DIAMETER_SUCCESS_SERVER_NAME_NOT_STORED). That single code is standing in for
two quite different outcomes -- an identity the HSS has never heard of, and one
it holds but that nobody has registered -- and it is the wrong code for either.
TS 29.229 6.2 lists 2004 among the *success* codes and TS 29.228 6.1.4 does not
name it as an LIA result at all; the two it does name are
DIAMETER_ERROR_USER_UNKNOWN (5001) for an unknown identity and
DIAMETER_ERROR_IDENTITY_NOT_REGISTERED (5003) for a known but unregistered one.

What this costs is visible at the I-CSCF. ims_icscf's LIA handler maps 5001 to
604 "Does Not Exist Anywhere", 5003 to 480 and 2003 to a successful
unregistered-service selection, and has nothing for 2004, so it takes its
default branch:

    SIP/2.0 500 Forbidden - HSS responded with unknown Experimental Result Code

A call to a number this HSS does not have therefore comes back to the UE as a
server error, which reads as a fault in the core rather than as the correct
"no such subscriber". Measured on the Cx interface, the LIR/LIA pair for an
unprovisioned number matched on hop-by-hop identifier:

    LIR  Public-Identity = sip:35900000002@ims.<realm>
    LIA  Result-Code = <absent>  Experimental-Result-Code = 2004

The same 2004 also defeats ims_icscf's route_lir_user_unknown modparam, which
exists precisely so a deployment can re-target an unknown number -- to a BGCF,
a trunk, a lookup of its own -- instead of rejecting it. That hook is keyed on
5001 and so never fires against this HSS, which makes the behaviour impossible
to work around in configuration and is why the fix belongs here.

Telling the two cases apart needs no new lookup: hss_cx_get_user_name() returns
the IMPI associated with the IMPU, and NULL when there is none. The UAR path in
this same file already uses it that way, for the same distinction and with the
same 5001 on failure, so this makes the LIR agree with the UAR rather than
introducing a new convention.

One limit is worth stating, because it decides which of the two codes a given
number gets. The HSS's Cx registry is built from registration activity -- an
IMPU enters it in the MAR path and again when a SAR downloads the profile --
and not from the subscriber database, so an identity that is provisioned but
has never registered is absent from it and answers 5001, the same as a number
the network does not serve at all. 5003 is what a public identity gets once the
HSS has seen it and it has no S-CSCF at the moment of the query. Telling
"provisioned but never registered" apart from "not provisioned" would mean a
database lookup on every LIR, which this does not add.

Either way the I-CSCF now has a code it can map, and the UE gets 604 or 480
rather than 500.
diff --git a/src/hss/hss-cx-path.c b/src/hss/hss-cx-path.c
index fb4e399..a9c72d6 100644
--- a/src/hss/hss-cx-path.c
+++ b/src/hss/hss-cx-path.c
@@ -1613,8 +1613,21 @@ static int hss_ogs_diam_cx_lir_cb(struct msg **msg, struct avp *avp,
     /* Get Server-Name by IMPU(Public-Identity) */
     server_name = hss_cx_get_server_name(public_identity);
     if (!server_name) {
-        ogs_error("No Server-Name in Public-Identity[%s]", public_identity);
-        result_code = OGS_DIAM_CX_SERVER_NAME_NOT_STORED;
+        /*
+         * Having no S-CSCF for the identity is two different answers, and
+         * TS 29.228 6.1.4 keeps them apart: DIAMETER_ERROR_USER_UNKNOWN when
+         * the HSS does not hold the public identity at all, and
+         * DIAMETER_ERROR_IDENTITY_NOT_REGISTERED when it does but nothing is
+         * registered against it. The UAR path above already distinguishes the
+         * two the same way, by whether an IMPI is associated with the IMPU.
+         */
+        if (!hss_cx_get_user_name(public_identity)) {
+            ogs_error("Unknown Public-Identity[%s]", public_identity);
+            result_code = OGS_DIAM_CX_ERROR_USER_UNKNOWN;
+        } else {
+            ogs_error("No Server-Name in Public-Identity[%s]", public_identity);
+            result_code = OGS_DIAM_CX_ERROR_IDENTITY_NOT_REGISTERED;
+        }
         error_occurred = 1;
         goto out;
     }
